  "description": "<div>\n  <p>There are n numbered cities and directed flights <code>[from, to, price]</code>. Find the minimum total price from src to dst using at most k intermediate cities (that is, at most k + 1 flights). Return -1 if the destination cannot be reached within this limit. If src equals dst, the zero-flight journey costs 0.</p>\n  <p>Flight prices are positive, but the cheapest route with unlimited flights can violate the stop limit. Account for the flight count as well as the accumulated price.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3, flights = [[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[0,2,500]], src = 0, dst = 2, k = 1\n<strong>Output:</strong> 200\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> The two-flight route costs 200 and uses one stop.</pre>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3, flights = [[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[0,2,500]], src = 0, dst = 2, k = 0\n<strong>Output:</strong> 500\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> With no stops only the direct flight is permitted.</pre>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3, flights = [[0,1,20]], src = 0, dst = 2, k = 2\n<strong>Output:</strong> -1\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> No flight reaches city 2.</pre>\n\n  <p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>1 &lt;= n &lt;= 30</code>; city numbers are <code>0</code> through <code>n-1</code>.</li>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= flights.length &lt;= 100</code>; each flight has three integers and distinct endpoints.</li>\n    <li>There is at most one flight for each ordered pair of cities; <code>1 &lt;= price &lt;= 1000</code>.</li>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= src, dst &lt; n</code>; <code>0 &lt;= k &lt; n</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>",
